<interrogationes unitas="mensura">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I record a measurement during a build without slowing the build down?
    <responsum symbolum="mensura_annotare mensura_condere mensura_sessionem_novam mensura_id_novum mensura_momentum_nunc">Fill
      a Mensura (sessio from mensura_sessionem_novam, id from
      mensura_id_novum, momentum from mensura_momentum_nunc, a
      titulus, a valor and a unitas such as "secunda") and append it
      with mensura_annotare: one TSV line in a plain diary file, no
      sqlite. After the run, mensura_condere moves the whole diary
      into the volumen in one transaction and then truncates the
      diary.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does mensura write to a diary file first instead of straight into the volumen?
    <responsum symbolum="mensura_annotare mensura_condere" via="lib/mensura.worklog.md">A
      sqlite write opens the database and commits a transaction,
      perhaps ten milliseconds. Across CL files that adds about a
      second to the very build being measured, and an instrument that
      changes what it measures gets switched off. Appending a line is
      nearly free; the fold into the volumen happens once, after the
      run.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a measurement title or unit contain a tab or a newline?
    <responsum symbolum="mensura_ad_lineam mensura_annotare mensura_cursus_initium_ad_lineam">No.
      mensura_ad_lineam returns an empty chorda (and mensura_annotare
      FALSUM) when sessio, id, parens, titulus or unitas contains a
      TAB, LF or CR: a format that lets a field break it corrupts data
      silently, so it refuses instead of escaping. Run lines are the
      same, except that argv and nota are escaped (\\ \t \n \r) because
      a command is data.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I fold a diary with mensura_condere while other processes are still appending to it?
    <responsum symbolum="mensura_condere" via="tools/mensor.c">Not
      safely: mensura_condere reads to the end, commits, then
      truncates the file, with no lock, so a line appended between the
      last read and the truncation is lost. bin/mensor's run fold
      (_cursus_condere) avoids this by renaming the diary first and
      folding the renamed copy (later appenders create a new file);
      'mensor condere' folds the live measurement diary directly and
      has the race. Truncation after success is what stops the next
      fold from counting everything twice.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does MensuraFructus report after mensura_condere?
    <responsum symbolum="MensuraFructus mensura_condere">successus,
      conditae (lines stored) and causa. successus is VERUM even when
      some lines were malformed or could not be appended: those are
      skipped and counted in causa ("N lineae malformatae
      praeteritae"), never silently dropped - but they are gone once
      the diary is truncated. successus is FALSUM if an argument is
      NIHIL, the diary cannot be opened, or the transaction cannot
      start or commit; then nothing is truncated. A failed truncation
      is NOT reported: successus stays VERUM and the next fold stores
      the same lines again.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a diary line be arbitrarily long?
    <responsum symbolum="mensura_condere mensura_cursus_initium_ad_lineam">No.
      mensura_condere reads lines with fgets into a buffer of IV *
      MXXIV bytes, so a longer line arrives in pieces and the pieces
      are counted malformed (then truncated away). The writers do not
      enforce this: nota is
      capped at CURSUS_NOTA_MAXIMA bytes, but argv is not capped at
      all, so a run started with a very long command line is lost at
      fold time.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I record a run with a start, an end and an exit code so killed runs still show up?
    <responsum symbolum="mensura_cursum_incipere mensura_cursum_finire mensura_cursus_legere CursusStatus">Write
      a CursusInitium with mensura_cursum_incipere when the run starts
      and a CursusFinis with the same id with mensura_cursum_finire
      when it ends; both are appended to the same diary. After
      mensura_condere, mensura_cursus_legere pairs them by id into a
      Xar of Cursus in start order: exit 0 is CURSUS_TRANSIIT, any
      other is CURSUS_FRACTUS, and a start with no end is
      CURSUS_INTERRUPTUS (killed, hung, or still running).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I keep the Mensura that mensura_ex_linea gives me after the line buffer is gone?
    <responsum symbolum="mensura_ex_linea mensura_cursus_ex_linea">No:
      its chorda fields point INTO the line you passed (nothing is
      copied), so keep the line alive or copy the fields. In
      mensura_cursus_ex_linea only argv and nota are new chordae in
      the piscina (they are unescaped); the other fields alias the
      line too. Note that mensura_ex_linea only rejects a valor or
      momentum field with no leading number: "1.5abc" reads as
      1.5.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I read stored measurements back out of the volumen?
    <responsum symbolum="mensura_mensuras_legere mensura_cursus_legere" imperium="bin/mensor quaestio">mensura_mensuras_legere(volumen,
      praefixum, piscina) returns a Xar of Mensura whose titulus starts
      with the prefix (an empty prefix gives all), in stored order;
      NIHIL if the volume cannot be read. It reads every event in the
      volume each call. For questions about runs (history, failures,
      trends) use the named queries of bin/mensor quaestio.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why is momentum an s64 and not an unsigned type?
    <responsum symbolum="mensura_momentum_nunc">The clock is
      gettimeofday, which is not monotonic: NTP can step it back, so
      the difference of two readings can be negative. A signed type
      shows that; an unsigned one would wrap it to a huge positive
      number and make a branch silently never run. For a run's
      duration CursusFinis carries duratio_ms from a monotonic
      clock.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
